1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,704
Incident channel, 19:40. Two guests
booking the Harborview Hotel on different
laptops somehow share a cart. Guest A
selects a king ocean-view; Guest B
refreshes and sees A's room with A's
loyalty rate.

2
00:00:13,823 --> 00:00:25,800
Support screenshots prove it. Engineering
finds BookingCart registered as the
default singleton — one instance for the
whole JVM — stuffed into a @Controller
that was never meant to hold per-user
state.

3
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:39,695
Scope is the answer to "how many
instances, and over what boundary?"
Spring's built-in scopes include singleton
(one per container), prototype (new
instance every retrieval), and, in web-
aware contexts, request, session,

4
00:00:39,816 --> 00:00:41,904
and application.

5
00:00:42,024 --> 00:00:55,079
Default is singleton. That default is
correct for stateless services and wrong
for a mutable cart. Look at this java for
a moment. Runtime for session scope with a
scoped proxy.

6
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:02,615
On context refresh Spring does not put one
shared BookingCart into the controller. It
injects a proxy.

7
00:01:02,736 --> 00:01:13,032
On each HTTP call, when the controller
touches cart.add, the proxy resolves the
real cart for the current HTTP session —
creating one on first use — and delegates.

8
00:01:13,152 --> 00:01:23,376
Guest A's session id maps to cart instance
A; Guest B gets cart instance B. When the
session expires, that cart becomes
eligible for destruction callbacks.

9
00:01:23,495 --> 00:01:30,311
Singleton ReservationService stays one
shared instance, which is what you want
for a stateless booking API client.

10
00:01:30,431 --> 00:01:38,783
Prototype scope would create a new cart on
every getBean or injection point
resolution — usually wrong for a cart you
mutate across several requests.

11
00:01:38,903 --> 00:01:46,655
Request scope lives for one HTTP request
only; useful for per-request buffers,
useless for a multi-step booking wizard.

12
00:01:46,775 --> 00:01:49,679
Misconception unique to scopes:

13
00:01:49,799 --> 00:02:02,111
"Annotating @Scope("session") without a
proxy is enough when injecting into a
singleton." If a singleton controller
receives a session-scoped object without
proxyMode (or ObjectFactory/Provider),

14
00:02:02,231 --> 00:02:08,687
it may capture one session's instance at
injection time and share it forever — the
Harborview bug in a tuxedo.

15
00:02:08,807 --> 00:02:15,503
The proxy (or lookup API) re-resolves per
call. Carts are isolated again.

16
00:02:15,623 --> 00:02:25,415
The next outage is different: Redis
connections for rate caching are created
lazily on the first booking spike after
deploy, and the pool warms under traffic
instead of before it.

17
00:02:25,536 --> 00:02:29,448
Creation timing — not instance count — is
now the problem.
